Build a Validation Checklist and Use It

The Story
CityCare Clinic has 12 doctors, 6 receptionists, and about 400 patients per week. Appointments
are booked by phone and paper diary, so double bookings and long waits happen often.
A software company has written a requirements document (SRS) for MediTrack, an online
appointment system. The clinic is nervous about signing the contract, so they have hired your
pair as consultants to check the document first.
Your job: build a checklist, use it on the document, and find the problems.
Who uses the system: Patient, Doctor, Receptionist, Clinic administrator, Data protection
officer.
The clinic manager’s worries:
 Double bookings must never happen.
 Patients forget appointments, which costs money.
 Medical information is private.
 Elderly patients and emergency walk-ins must not be left out.
 Monday mornings are very busy.
The Document You Will Check
Save this as srs/meditrack-srs.md.
R-01 The system shall allow patients to book appointments online.
R-02 The system shall respond quickly to booking requests.
R-03 The system shall prevent double bookings.
R-04 Each appointment shall last 15 minutes.
R-05 The system shall allow doctors to set appointment length between 10 and 30 minutes.
R-06 The system shall send reminders to patients before appointments.
R-07 Patients may cancel appointments at any time and the system shall update the doctor’s
schedule and notify the receptionist.
R-08 The system shall be user-friendly for all age groups.
R-09 Patient records shall be stored securely.
R-10 Receptionists shall be able to book appointments for walk-in patients.
R-11 Patients may book up to 3 active appointments.
R-12 The system shall be available during normal working hours.
R-13 Overdue payments shall be handled as per clinic policy.
R-14 Doctors shall be able to view their daily schedule.

What Is a Checklist?
A checklist is a list of yes/no questions you answer by reading a document. Each answer is Yes,
No, or N/A.
Rules for Every Checklist Item
Every item must pass four tests (AOUT):
Letter Test Ask yourself
A Atomic Is it only one question? (No “and”.)
O Observable Can I answer by looking at the document?
U Unambiguous Would my partner give the same answer?
T Traceable Do I know why this question exists?
Also:
 “Yes” must always be the good answer.
 Write 15 to 20 items.
Example:
 Bad: “Is security good?”
 Good: “Does the document say which roles may view a patient’s medical record?”
Part A: Build the Checklist
Create checklist/v0.1.md with 15 to 20 items. Get your ideas from three places:
1. Rules of a good document: Is it clear? Testable? Free of contradictions? Complete?
2. Risks: What could go wrong at this clinic? Find at least 5 risks.
3. People: If I were each of the 5 users, what would I look for in this document? Write at least one
item for each user.
Required table columns:
Markdown
ID Item Answer Evidence Where question
came from

Part B: Use the Checklist
Alone (30 minutes), no talking to your partner:

 Student A and Student B each answer the checklist separately on the SRS.
 Save your work as runs/run-A.md and runs/run-B.md.
 Write Yes, No, or N/A for every item.
 For every “No”, write the requirement ID and a one-line reason.
 Mark any item you were unsure about with “?”.
 Write down your start time and end time.
Together (20 minutes):
 Compare your answers.
 Agree on the real problems in the document.
 List them in findings.md.
Example (do not copy): R-02 says “respond quickly”. A checklist question could be “Does
every speed requirement have a number and a unit?” The answer is No, because “quickly” has no
number.
